---
layout: "../layouts/PhilosophyLayout.astro";

const volumes = [
  {
    title: "Volume I",
    name: "Latent Form Theory",
    subtitle: "The Philosophy of Essential Subtraction",
    chapters: [
      "The Nature of Form",
      "Essential Subtraction",
      "Composition and Necessity",
      "The Role of the Maker"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Volume II",
    name: "Modern Stratigraphy",
    subtitle: "Material Memory and the Organization of Earth",
    chapters: [
      "Matter Before Image",
      "Deep Time and Material Memory",
      "Selection as Composition",
      "Authenticity and Material Honesty",
      "The Nexus of Timescales"
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Volume III",
    name: "The Disciplined Eye",
    subtitle: "Perception, Judgment, and the Ethics of Seeing",
    chapters: [
      "Seeing Versus Looking",
      "Taste and Judgment",
      "Constraint",
      "Silence",
      "Stewardship",
      "The Maker"
    ]
  }
];
---

<div id="intro" class="scroll-anchor"></div>

<div class="theory-layout">

  <aside class="sidebar">

    <h3>Latent Form Theory</h3>

    <nav>
      <a href="#overview">Overview</a>

      {volumes.map((volume) => (
        <section>
          <strong>{volume.title}</strong>
          <a href={`#${volume.title.toLowerCase().replace(" ", "-")}`}>
            {volume.name}
          </a>

          <ul>
            {volume.chapters.map((chapter) => (
              <li>
                <a href="#">
                  {chapter}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>

        </section>
      ))}

    </nav>

  </aside>


  <main class="theory-content">

    <h1>Latent Form Theory</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Latent Form Theory is a philosophy of making organized around the
      recognition of inherent order. It argues that meaningful creation does
      not begin with invention, but with perception — the ability to recognize
      what already exists within material, structure, and possibility.
    </p>


    <section id="overview">

      <h2>Overview</h2>

      <p>
        Every creative work begins with abundance: countless possible forms,
        directions, and outcomes. Through disciplined choice, the maker removes
        what is unnecessary until the remaining form achieves coherence,
        balance, and necessity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory examines the relationship between material,
        perception, and intervention. It proposes that the maker's role is not
        to impose arbitrary intention upon the world, but to reveal and
        organize relationships that already exist.
      </p>

    </section>


    <section id="volume-i">

      <h2>Volume I: Latent Form Theory</h2>

      <h3>The Philosophy of Essential Subtraction</h3>

      <p>
        Volume I establishes the foundation of the philosophy: form emerges
        through disciplined reduction. Creation is not merely the addition of
        elements, but the elimination of what obscures essential structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The maker's responsibility is not to ask what can be added, but what
        must remain.
      </p>

    </section>


    <section id="volume-ii">

      <h2>Volume II: Modern Stratigraphy</h2>

      <h3>Material Memory and the Organization of Earth</h3>

      <p>
        Modern Stratigraphy explores the history already contained within
        natural materials. Wood, stone, mineral pigments, and earth are not
        passive resources but records of geological and biological processes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Matter is not empty before human intervention. Every material arrives
        carrying evidence of the forces that shaped it.
      </p>

    </section>


    <section id="volume-iii">

      <h2>Volume III: The Disciplined Eye</h2>

      <h3>Perception, Judgment, and the Ethics of Seeing</h3>

      <p>
        The final volume examines the faculty required to recognize latent
        order: disciplined perception.
      </p>

      <p>
        Seeing is not passive observation. It is an acquired ability to
        distinguish necessity from excess, composition from addition, and
        meaningful restraint from absence.
      </p>

    </section>


    <section id="conclusion">

      <h2>The Complete Framework</h2>

      <p>
        Together, the three volumes form a complete philosophy of creative
        practice:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Method</strong> — the discipline of subtraction.</li>
        <li><strong>Matter</strong> — the recognition of history within material.</li>
        <li><strong>Maker</strong> — the cultivation of perception and judgment.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory ultimately argues that creation is not the act of
        bringing something from nothing. It is the disciplined practice of
        recognizing, revealing, and carrying forward the order already present.
      </p>

    </section>


  </main>

</div>


